{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310753,"nonce":"NeDZiuNhbZvn84VaxW5hM+oDT98=","title":"WASM cache poisoning FIXED (59d9a74) + the SEPARATE real wasm degenerate-output bug (pre-existing forward/template path, not matmul)","message":"Two distinct issues untangled 2026-06-13 (the first was masking the second).\n\n**(1) BLADE cache native/wasm collision — FIXED, commit 59d9a74.** The cache keyed `.blade` files by source PATH only, so the same `.hx` lowered for native vs wasm (different MIR via `#if wasm`, host-import vs native-runtime bindings) shared one slot. Running native then wasm (or vice versa) served wrong-target MIR until `.rayzor` was deleted by hand. This produced FALSE symptoms I initially misdiagnosed as compiler bugs: \"missing meta key 'general.architecture'\" load trap + a phantom \"GGUFReader constructor never runs / duplicate haxe_stringmap_* forward-ref stub\" theory. ALL WRONG — it was native MIR (with native StringMap bindings) poisoning the wasm build's cache. Fix: `CompilationConfig::cache_discriminator()` folds sorted `extra_defines` into the cache dir → `.rayzor/blade/cache/native` vs `.../wasm-XXXXXXXX`, fully separate trees. Also threaded `--no-cache` through `run --wasm` (cmd_run_wasm dropped the flag; compile hardcoded enable_cache=true). User no longer needs manual `rm .rayzor`. Verified: native-then-wasm keeps wasm metadata correct; `--no-cache` → enable_cache=false. **Lesson: ALWAYS run wasm with `--no-cache` (or after rm .rayzor) when debugging, and compare against native, before theorizing a compiler bug.**\n\n**(2) The REAL wasm degenerate-output bug — FIXED 2026-06-13, commit 61829ab.** Three stacked bugs in the wasm ATTENTION path (each masking the next), found by instrumenting forwardIds/TransformerBlock/GQAttention with per-op `sum()` checksums and diffing wasm vs native (divergence was block-0 attnOut=0). (a) `rayzor_tensor_bmm` (runtime-wasm tensor.rs) was a stub forwarding to 2D `rayzor_tensor_matmul` (rejects ndim!=3) → null for the batched Q·Kᵀ/attn·V whose inputs are permute/transpose VIEWS → scores all zero. Fixed: real STRIDED batched matmul. (b) `rayzor_tensor_softmax_inplace_f32` (kernels.rs): wasm `libm::expf` TRAPS (`unreachable`) on the f32::NEG_INFINITY causal-mask cells where native `x.exp()` returns 0. Fixed: inline stable softmax guarding non-finite exp args. (c) `rayzor_tensor_reshape` (tensor.rs) did `if !is_contiguous { return 0 }` → `context.permute([1,0,2]).reshape(...)` (strided view) returned null → contextFlat=0 → attention output zero → residual passed input through → degenerate. Fixed: materialise strided source to contiguous row-major first. Result: block-0 matches native (51.5 vs 51.2); coherent output (\"The capital of France is Paris.\", \"Blue.\", \"2 + 2 = 4\") matching native. wasm-only; native unaffected (36.6 tok/s). LESSON: the wasm tensor ops that take VIEWS (bmm, reshape, and watch transpose/permute consumers) were stubs/contiguous-only — audit all runtime-wasm ops for strided-view handling.\n\n**(historical) The degenerate-output bug — was OPEN, pre-existing, NOT the matmul.** On a clean `--no-cache` compile, wasm llama-chat is still degenerate vs native:\n- \"capital of France?\" → wasm \"The best answer is :\" / native \"The capital of France is Paris.\"\n- \"Name a color.\" → wasm \"The best answer is: 1.\" / native \"Blue.\"\n- \"2+2?\" → wasm \"The best answer is: 2)\" / native \"2 + 2 = 4\"\nWASM always emits the \"The best answer is:\" prefix with slight prompt-dependent tails. **NOT the matmul:** `RAYZOR_WASM_NO_HOST_MATMUL=1` (guest sequential wasm-SIMD matmul, none of b50b851's code) gives the IDENTICAL degenerate output — so both matmul paths agree, the bug is upstream/elsewhere in the shared wasm forward path. Token 1 (\"The\") matches native; divergence starts token 2. **Prime suspect: the chat-template / special-token / tokenizer path** (Main.hx ~314-326 `tok.specialId(\"\u003c|start_header_id|\u003e\")` etc. carry W0014 \"cross-context iface return resolved late\" warnings) producing a malformed instruction context → the \"The best answer is:\" attractor (looks like the model answering a malformed/multiple-choice-style prompt). Could also be BPE tokenization of the prompt on wasm. NEXT: dump the wasm-tokenized prompt token-ids + the special-token ids and compare to native; check GGUFTokenizer + the Llama-3 template build on wasm. Reliable clean repro now exists (`--no-cache`). Matmul perf (b50b851) is moot until this is fixed but is itself sound. See [[perf_wasm_host_parallel_matmul]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_stringmap_dup_stub` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}